      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Inhaled fluticasone propionate (FP) is a relatively new inhaled corticosteroid for the treatment of asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To assess efficacy and safety outcomes in studies that compared FP to placebo for treatment of chronic asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SEARCH STRATEGY",
          "content": "We searched the Cochrane Airways Group Specialised Register (January 2008), reference lists of articles, contacted trialists and searched abstracts of major respiratory society meetings (1997-2006)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SELECTION CRITERIA",
          "content": "Randomised trials in children and adults comparing FP to placebo in the treatment of chronic asthma. Two reviewers independently assessed articles for inclusion and risk of bias."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA COLLECTION AND ANALYSIS",
          "content": "Two review authors extracted data. Quantitative analyses were undertaken using Review Manager software."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Eighty-six studies met the inclusion criteria, recruiting 16,160 participants. In non-oral steroid treated asthmatics with mild and moderate disease FP resulted in improvements from baseline compared with placebo across all dose ranges (100 to 1000 mcg/d) in FEV1 (between 0.1 to 0.43 litres); morning PEF (between 23 and 46 L/min); symptom scores (based on a standardised scale, between 0.44 and 0.7); reduction in rescue beta-2 agonist use (between 1 and 1.4 puffs/day). High dose FP increased the number of patients who could withdraw from prednisolone: FP 1000-1500 mcg/day Peto Odds Ratio 14.07 (95% CI 7.17 to 27.57). FP at all doses led to a greater likelihood of sore throat, hoarseness and oral Candidiasis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "AUTHORS' CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Doses of FP in the range 100-1000 mcg/day are effective. In most patients with mild-moderate asthma improvements with low dose FP are only a little less than those associated with high doses when compared with placebo. High dose FP appears to have worthwhile oral-corticosteroid reducing properties. FP use is accompanied by an increased likelihood of oropharyngeal side effects."
        }
      ]},
